# LeetCode-provided API (practice files)

For problems where the platform implements a black-box API (`guess`, `isBadVersion`, `read4`, etc.):

1. Solver class: **`class Solution extends ApiClass`** (Java/TS) or the same **shape** as the LeetCode editor. Do **not** use the **file-named** class as the solver.
2. Do **not** add `private final int pick` / `firstBad`, a constructor that stores the secret, or `main` that wires a secret—LeetCode never exposes that; it confuses learners.
3. Do **not** implement the API method. At the **bottom** of the file (after `Solution`), add a **minimal stub** class with an **empty** API method body so `Solution extends ApiClass` and calls compile. One **`//`** line above the stub: LeetCode provides it; learner does not implement it.
4. The **commented reference** under the spacer contains **only** the **solver** method body—not a reference implementation of the API.
5. Linter “must return a value” on empty `guessNumber` / empty stub **API** is expected until the learner fills the solver.

## Example (Java)

```java
class Solution extends GuessGame {
    public int guessNumber(int n) {
        //
        // ...40-60 line spacer...
        //
        // int lo = 1, hi = n;
        // while (lo <= hi) { ... guess(mid) ... }
        // return -1;
    }
}

// LeetCode provides this class behind the scenes. Local stub only; you do not implement it.
class GuessGame {
    int guess(int num) {
    }
}
```
